<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (The Philolaic system presents two categories of problems. First, the Counter-Earth appears to serve no explanatory function beyond numerology and the explanation of eclipses, and there is no way to verify its existence empirically; second, how is the motion of the universe to be explained?)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (While for the first issue one might raise the objection that the empirical verification of astronomical theories was almost never the guiding compass of Greek astronomers, the second creates problems even from a purely philosophical standpoint: is the motion of the universe single, or are there additional motions proper to individual planets? Does the sphere of the fixed stars move everything except the Earth, which possesses its own proper motion? And what speed does the sphere of the fixed stars possess?)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 105 0 R
/E (If the harmony of the spheres exists, these questions are not merely technical in character, but take on a moral dimension, since every body participates in the harmony of the cosmos in which we are immersed.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
